<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Tairona</h1><p class="st-meta">200 CE – 1600 CE (approx.) · capital Teyuna</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Chiefdoms of Colombia's Sierra Nevada de Santa Marta that built stone-terraced towns such as the Ciudad Perdida and whose descendants still live in the mountains.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Settlement in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-nevada-de-santa-marta">Sierra Nevada de Santa Marta</a> and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> coast developed from around 200 CE, and the Tairona style, with stone architecture, emerged by about 1000 CE. Communities built towns on mountain ridges with terraces, stairways and paved paths.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Tairona were organised in independent chiefdoms and towns linked by alliances, with priests called mamas holding great authority. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ciudad-perdida">Teyuna</a>, known as the Ciudad Perdida or Lost City, had some 170 stone terraces and a network of paved roads. Farming of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassava">cassava</a> and fruit on terraces sustained dense populations. Their goldsmiths made fine pendants and nose ornaments in tumbaga, a gold-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> alloy.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Spanish contact began after the foundation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-marta">Santa Marta</a> in 1525. The Tairona resisted for decades until a major revolt was crushed around 1600, after which towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ciudad-perdida">Teyuna</a> were abandoned. Their descendants, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kogi-people">Kogi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arhuaco-people">Arhuaco</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wiwa-people">Wiwa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kankuamo-people">Kankuamo peoples</a>, still live in the Sierra.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/tairona.jpg" alt="Tairona" width="320" height="363"><figcaption>Image: Breogan2008 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Territorio_dos_taironas.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>200 CE – 1600 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Teyuna</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Tairona religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Chibchan</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Colombia</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Tayrona, Tairona confederation</dd></dl></aside></div>

